Sharpe Ratio: 0.0550
The Sharpe ratio measures the risk-adjusted return of a strategy. It is calculated as the ratio of the strategy's excess return (return above a risk-free rate) to its standard deviation (a measure of risk).
A Sharpe ratio of 0.0550 is quite low, indicating that the returns you gained are not well compensated relative to the risk taken. Typically, a Sharpe ratio of 1 or above is considered good, and anything below 0.5 is weak. In your case, the strategy has delivered returns, but it involved a lot of risk or volatility in doing so.

Sortino Ratio: 0.0023
The Sortino ratio is similar to the Sharpe ratio but focuses only on downside risk (i.e., it penalizes negative volatility, not total volatility).
A Sortino ratio of 0.0023 is also quite low, indicating that the strategy has not performed well in managing the downside risk (losses). A higher Sortino ratio would indicate that the strategy avoids large losses or negative returns.

